def build(make):
 return make(1620,'','Multisystem Processes & Disorders',['Biochemistry','Genetics','Metabolism'],
 'A 10-month-old boy develops lethargy and tachypnea during a viral illness. Laboratory studies show hyperammonemia, lactic acidosis, hypoglycemia, and urinary metabolites suggesting impaired activity of several mitochondrial carboxylases. Biotinidase activity is normal. Genetic testing identifies biallelic loss-of-function variants in CA5A, which encodes mitochondrial carbonic anhydrase VA.',
 'Which molecule is normally generated by the deficient enzyme to support the affected mitochondrial pathways?',
 {'A':'NADPH','B':'Biotin','C':'N-acetylglutamate','D':'Tetrahydrofolate','E':'Bicarbonate'},
 'E','Mitochondrial carbonic anhydrase VA generates bicarbonate that is supplied to CPS1 and multiple mitochondrial carboxylases',
 'Carbonic anhydrase VA catalyzes mitochondrial hydration of carbon dioxide to bicarbonate. The locally generated bicarbonate is required by carbamoyl phosphate synthetase 1 in the urea cycle and by mitochondrial biotin-dependent carboxylases including pyruvate carboxylase, propionyl-CoA carboxylase, and 3-methylcrotonyl-CoA carboxylase. CA5A deficiency can therefore produce simultaneous hyperammonemia, lactic acidosis, hypoglycemia, and a multiple-carboxylase biochemical pattern.',
 {'A':'NADPH is not the product generated by mitochondrial carbonic anhydrase VA and is not the common substrate defect linking CPS1 with the affected carboxylases.','B':'Biotin is a cofactor for the mitochondrial carboxylases but not for CPS1, and carbonic anhydrase VA does not synthesize biotin.','C':'N-acetylglutamate activates CPS1 but is not required by pyruvate, propionyl-CoA, or 3-methylcrotonyl-CoA carboxylases and is not generated by carbonic anhydrase.','D':'Tetrahydrofolate participates in one-carbon metabolism and is not the molecule supplied by carbonic anhydrase VA to these mitochondrial enzymes.','E':'Correct. Carbonic anhydrase VA generates intramitochondrial bicarbonate used by CPS1 and several bicarbonate-dependent carboxylases.'},
 'Recognize CA5A deficiency as impaired mitochondrial bicarbonate generation, simultaneously compromising CPS1 and several biotin-dependent carboxylases.',
 [{'source_id':'Q1620-PUBMED-PRIMARY','title':'Mitochondrial carbonic anhydrase VA deficiency resulting from CA5A alterations presents with hyperammonemia in early childhood','agency':'PubMed, U.S. National Library of Medicine','url':'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24530203/','source_section_date':None,'date_basis':'PubMed record live reverified 2026-09-18; no unsupported exact publication date inferred.','retrieved_at':'2026-09-18','section_locator':'impaired provision of bicarbonate to the four enzymes that participate in key pathways in intermediary metabolism'}],
 'B','Biotin is the strongest alternative because three affected enzymes are biotin-dependent carboxylases. It cannot explain the CPS1 defect, however, and CA-VA specifically generates bicarbonate rather than biotin; the primary study identifies impaired mitochondrial bicarbonate provision as the shared lesion.')
